{"study_accession":"PRJNA1092569","secondary_study_accession":"SRP498110","title":"Extracellular Vesicle miR-1 Delivery from Skeletal Muscle to Adipose Tissue Modifies Lipolytic Pathways in Response to Resistance Exercise in Humans","description":"Extracellular vesicles (EVs) have emerged as important mediators of inter-tissue signaling and exercise adaptations. In this human study (n=32), muscle-specific microRNA-1 (miR-1) was delivered to adipose tissue via EVs following an acute bout of resistance exercise. Using a multi-model machine learning automation tool, we discovered muscle primary miR-1 transcript and CD63+ EV count in circulation as top explanatory features for changes in adipose miR-1 levels in response to resistance exercise. RNA-sequencing (RNA-seq) and in-silico prediction of miR-1 target genes identified CAV2 and TRIM6 as miR-1 target genes upregulated in adipose tissue of participants with high increases in miR-1 levels following resistance exercise. Overexpression of miR-1 in differentiated human adipocyte-derived stem cells downregulated these miR-1 targets and enhanced catecholamine-induced lipolysis. These data identify a novel mechanism by which skeletal muscle communicates to adipose tissue and modulates lipolysis via delivery of miR-1-containing EVs. Overall design: We performed RNA-sequencing of human adipose tissue following a single bout of resistance exercise.","pubmed":"39316445","geo_series":"","n_samples":12,"samples":[{"sample_accession":"SAMN40627625","study_accession":"PRJNA1092569","blood_class":"plasma_serum_cfrna","class_from":"study","title":"Low miR-1, replicate 6, RNA-seq","description":"Low miR-1, replicate 6, RNA-seq","sex":"","geo_sample":"GSM8171926","access":"open","dbgap":"","n_runs":1,"first_public":"2024-10-16","attributes":[["source_name","Adipose tissue"],["tissue","Adipose tissue"],["treatment","Resistance exercise"],["geo_loc_name","missing"],["collection_date","missing"]],"runs":[{"run_accession":"SRR28470327","e